Here is the JavaScript program to solve your problem. 

In JavaScript, standard array search methods like `.includes()` or `.indexOf()` use **strict equality** (`===`), meaning they check both the value and the data type. Since your array contains numbers but your search element is a string (`"3"`), a strict check will return `false`. 

To handle cases where the types might be different, we can use the `.some()` method with **loose equality** (`==`), which converts the types before comparing them.

### JavaScript Solution

```javascript
const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1];
const searchElement = "3";

/**
 * Checks if an element exists in an array.
 * Uses loose equality (==) to handle different data types (e.g., string vs number).
 */
function checkElementExists(arr, element) {
  // .some() iterates through the array and returns true if at least one 
  // element matches the condition (item == element)
  return arr.some(item => item == element);
}

/**
 * Checks if an element exists strictly.
 * Uses strict equality (===) where both value and type must match.
 */
function checkElementStrict(arr, element) {
  return arr.includes(element);
}

// 1. Loose Comparison (Handles the type difference)
const existsLoosely = checkElementExists(array, searchElement);
console.log(`Does "${searchElement}" exist (ignoring type)?`, existsLoosely); 
// Output: true

// 2. Strict Comparison (Fails because Number !== String)
const existsStrictly = checkElementStrict(array, searchElement);
console.log(`Does "${searchElement}" exist (strict type)?`, existsStrictly); 
// Output: false
```

### Explanation of the Code:
1. **`arr.some(item => item == element)`**: This is the best approach for your specific constraint. It loops through the array and checks if the current item is loosely equal (`==`) to the search element. When it hits the number `3`, JavaScript coerces the string `"3"` into a number, evaluates `3 == 3` as `true`, and stops searching. (Duplicates do not affect this method).
2. **`arr.includes(element)`**: This is the modern standard for finding elements in an array, but it uses strict equality (`===`). It will return `false` because the number `3` and the string `"3"` are not exactly the same. Including this demonstrates how to properly handle type-sensitive versus type-insensitive searches.

The best and most modern way to build a Java web application is by using **Spring Boot**. It greatly simplifies the setup process and removes the need for complex XML configurations.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide to creating a user registration application using **Spring Boot**, **Spring Data JPA** (for database operations), **H2 Database** (an in-memory database for easy testing), and **Thymeleaf** (for the HTML frontend).

### Step 1: Set up the project
Go to [Spring Initializr](https://start.spring.io/) and generate a project with the following settings:
*   **Project:** Maven
*   **Language:** Java
*   **Dependencies:** Spring Web, Thymeleaf, Spring Data JPA, H2 Database
*   Click **Generate**, download the `.zip`, extract it, and open it in your IDE (IntelliJ, Eclipse, or VS Code).

---

### Step 2: Create the User Entity (Model)
This class represents the data we want to save to the database.
Create a file named `User.java`:

```java
package com.example.registration.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String name;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String email;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String password;

    // Default constructor
    public User() {}

    // Getters and Setters
    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public void setId(Long id) { this.id = id; }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public String getEmail() { return email; }
    public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = email; }

    public String getPassword() { return password; }
    public void setPassword(String password) { this.password = password; }
}
```

---

### Step 3: Create the Repository
This interface handles saving and retrieving users from the database.
Create a file named `UserRepository.java`:

```java
package com.example.registration.repository;

import com.example.registration.model.User;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    // Spring Data JPA automatically provides methods like save(), findAll(), etc.
}
```

---

### Step 4: Create the Controller
This class handles HTTP requests (showing the form and processing the form submission).
Create a file named `UserController.java`:

```java
package com.example.registration.controller;

import com.example.registration.model.User;
import com.example.registration.repository.UserRepository;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;
import org.springframework.ui.Model;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ModelAttribute;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;

@Controller
public class UserController {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    // Show the registration form
    @GetMapping("/register")
    public String showRegistrationForm(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("user", new User());
        return "register"; // Looks for register.html
    }

    // Process the form submission
    @PostMapping("/register")
    public String processRegistration(@ModelAttribute("user") User user, Model model) {
        // Save user to database
        userRepository.save(user);

        model.addAttribute("name", user.getName());
        return "success"; // Looks for success.html
    }
}
```

---

### Step 5: Create the HTML Pages
Spring Boot looks for HTML files in the `src/main/resources/templates` folder.

**1. Create `register.html`** (The form)
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org">
<head>
    <title>Register</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>User Registration</h2>

    <!-- The form binds to the 'user' object sent from the Controller -->
    <form th:action="@{/register}" th:object="${user}" method="post">

        <label>Name:</label>
        <input type="text" th:field="*{name}" required /><br/><br/>

        <label>Email:</label>
        <input type="email" th:field="*{email}" required /><br/><br/>

        <label>Password:</label>
        <input type="password" th:field="*{password}" required /><br/><br/>

        <button type="submit">Register</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

**2. Create `success.html`** (The success page)
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org">
<head>
    <title>Registration Successful</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Registration Successful!</h2>
    <p>Welcome, <span th:text="${name}">User</span>! Your account has been created.</p>
    <a href="/register">Register another user</a>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### Step 6: Configure the Database
Open `src/main/resources/application.properties` and add the following lines to configure the H2 memory database:

```properties
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect
# Automatically creates the database tables based on your User class
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update 
```

---

### Step 7: Run the Application
Locate your main application class (usually named `RegistrationApplication.java` or similar) and run it. 

Once the application starts:
1. Open your web browser.
2. Go to `http://localhost:8080/register`
3. Fill out the form and click "Register".

### ⚠️ Crucial Security Note for Real Applications
In the code above, the password is saved as **plain text**. In a real-world application, you **must never do this**. You should use Spring Security to encrypt the password using `BCryptPasswordEncoder` before saving it to the database:

```java
// Example of what a real-world save looks like:
import org.springframework.security.crypto.bcrypt.BCryptPasswordEncoder;

// Inside your controller or service:
BCryptPasswordEncoder passwordEncoder = new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
String encodedPassword = passwordEncoder.encode(user.getPassword());
user.setPassword(encodedPassword);
userRepository.save(user);
```

